1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,777
RaceLab is new hackathon work by Tang Vu. It reuses
the Meta integration

2
00:00:06,777 --> 00:00:13,555
and local infrastructure from the earlier event
prototype, LiveWorld. A handler passes its

3
00:00:13,555 --> 00:00:20,332
sequential test. Two requests overlap, and it breaks.
RaceLab makes that order executable,

4
00:00:20,332 --> 00:00:21,896
visible, and repeatable.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,131
Here is the reservation handler. It reads stock,
checks availability, writes the new

6
00:00:30,131 --> 00:00:36,261
value, and records an order. Every operation runs in
QuickJS against an asynchronous

7
00:00:36,261 --> 00:00:42,392
simulated store. The scheduler pauses at store
operations. At this step, both buyers

8
00:00:42,392 --> 00:00:45,221
have read the same last seat.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,659
Advance the recorded order. Buyer A commits an order.
Buyer B writes from

10
00:00:53,659 --> 00:00:59,319
its earlier read and commits another. There are now
two orders against one

11
00:00:59,319 --> 00:01:04,978
seat. The independent checks reject the execution.
Re-execute starts from the initial store

12
00:01:04,978 --> 00:01:08,896
and reproduces the same failure, without asking the
model.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,920
Muse Spark receives the original source, contract,
and serial trace. It proposes the

14
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:23,839
overlapping schedule, reads the execution feedback,
and writes this replacement handler. In this

15
00:01:23,839 --> 00:01:29,759
retained run, it uses an exclusive lock around the
check, update, and receipt.

16
00:01:29,759 --> 00:01:35,679
The acceptance contract stays outside the submitted
module. A confident explanation cannot turn

17
00:01:35,679 --> 00:01:37,500
a failed check green.

18
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,704
The candidate passes eleven distinct schedules across
four input cases, including additional capacity

19
00:01:43,704 --> 00:01:49,408
and request counts. The display distinguishes a
completed finite sweep from a search

20
00:01:49,408 --> 00:01:55,112
that reached its cap. Export carries the source and
schedules. Opening a report

21
00:01:55,112 --> 00:01:59,500
recomputes the checks and treats imported model
attribution as unverified.

22
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,281
The same loop repairs duplicate webhook notifications
and a shared counter in the

23
00:02:05,281 --> 00:02:10,563
other two authored cases. The three retained
investigations used six Meta requests in

24
00:02:10,563 --> 00:02:15,844
total. Simple round-robin scheduling also exposes
these bugs. The demonstrated Meta contribution is

25
00:02:15,844 --> 00:02:19,500
the executed hypothesis and generated repair, with
independent feedback.

26
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,183
Changing the source clears stale results. A separate
live browser investigation on an

27
00:02:26,183 --> 00:02:32,366
edited handler completed the probe, patch, and check
loop. Recorded replay works without

28
00:02:32,366 --> 00:02:38,549
an API key. Timeline steps expose committed state.
Both original and candidate source

29
00:02:38,549 --> 00:02:39,500
remain inspectable.

30
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,183
This prototype covers JavaScript handlers using a
simulated store. Real databases, networks,
operating-system

31
00:02:46,183 --> 00:02:52,366
threads, and arbitrary repositories are outside its
scope. New workloads need explicit semantics

32
00:02:52,366 --> 00:02:58,549
and independent contracts. RaceLab makes an agent
repair an experiment you can replay

33
00:02:58,549 --> 00:02:59,500
and challenge.
